            # The two files are the same code with different quotes, so the
            # `src` comparison is rewritten once per quote style and the bug
            # is fixed on both dialogs rather than on the one the issue named.
            #
            # **Three comparisons are here and two of them are rewritten.**
            # All three were measured first, and the measurement is what
            # decided which:
            #
            # 1. `isInitialMedia`'s *other* branch, which uses
            #    `dataset.originalSrc` when the image has one, is rewritten
            #    too -- and the reason is **not** the shim. Neither the
            #    `setAttribute` wrapper nor `IN` touches
            #    `data-original-src`: the markup map above says in as many
            #    words that its attribute test is an exact name, so
            #    `data-original-src` is not a `data-src`. What prefixes it is
            #    the generic HTML location further down, whose
            #    `sub_filter 'src="/'` is a bare substring and matches inside
            #    `data-original-src="/...` -- no rule beside it claims that
            #    position first. So markup that reached the page as an **HTML
            #    response** carries a prefixed `data-original-src`, and Odoo
            #    ships that attribute inside stored arch (every themed image
            #    in `mass_mailing_themes/views/mass_mailing_themes_templates.xml`
            #    has one). On a field value, which arrives over `call_kw` and
            #    is not rewritten, the same attribute is root-relative. The
            #    branch therefore has to be prefix-agnostic rather than
            #    assumed clean, and it `return`s *before* the `src` line
            #    below, so rule 3 alone would not have reached the website
            #    editor's dialog. One pattern covers both dialogs here: this
            #    is the one line the two spell identically.
            # 2. `DocumentSelector.fetchAttachments`, which compares the
            #    element's `href` with `` `/web/content/${attachment.id}` ``,
            #    is already correct under Ingress -- and a strip on the `href`
            #    alone, which is what the issue asked for, would have *broken*
            #    it. That template literal begins `` `/web/ ``, which is one of
            #    the generic literal rules at the top of this location, so the
            #    left operand arrives at the browser already prefixed and the
            #    comparison is prefixed-against-prefixed. The coupling is not
            #    obvious from either side, so it is held by a test rather than
            #    by this comment: `test_ingress_media_dialog_preselect.py`
            #    serves the captured bundle bytes through a real nginx
            #    carrying this location's own literal rules, runs the
            #    comparison on what comes back, and refuses a `sub_filter`
            #    that names the `href` comparison at all. The same test
            #    serves a page-HTML sample through the generic location's
            #    rules, which is where rule 1's reason is executed.
            # 3. The `src` branch, the one the issue described, below.
            #
            # The three patterns below were derived on 2026-10-01 from the
            # pinned `.deb` through Odoo's own serve path. The first occurs
            # once in each dialog and so twice in a bundle that carries both,
            # which `sub_filter_once off` at the top of this location handles;
            # the other two occur once each in the whole package. Two more
            # were measured and left alone -- the document comparison in each
            # dialog; `tests/fixtures/bundles/README.md` carries all five with
            # their per-bundle counts.
            sub_filter 'if(this.props.media.dataset.originalSrc){return this.props.media.dataset.originalSrc===attachment.image_src;}' 'if(this.props.media.dataset.originalSrc){return (window.__WOOW_INGRESS_MARKUP_OUT__||function(v){return v})(this.props.media.dataset.originalSrc)===attachment.image_src;}';
            sub_filter 'return this.props.media.getAttribute("src")===attachment.image_src;' 'return (window.__WOOW_INGRESS_MARKUP_OUT__||function(v){return v})(this.props.media.getAttribute("src"))===attachment.image_src;';
            sub_filter "return this.props.media.getAttribute('src')===attachment.image_src;" "return (window.__WOOW_INGRESS_MARKUP_OUT__||function(v){return v})(this.props.media.getAttribute('src'))===attachment.image_src;";
            # The code view's way back off (issue #240; ADR 0004's fifth
            # 2026-10-01 postscript has the decision, this comment has what an
            # editor of the one line below needs). It is the **sixth** markup
            # insertion of `html_editor`'s own field and the last item on the
            # first postscript's open list: `toggleCodeView` assigns the
            # record's value straight into the editable when the code view goes
            # back off, so under Ingress every root-relative URL in the record
            # was fetched from the Home Assistant root again -- the same 404 as
            # #210's two onboarding pictures, on the same field, reached a
            # different way.
            #
            # **`IN_VALUE` and not `IN`, for #237's reason.** `get value()`
            # returns `markup(newVal)` whenever the record's value is a
            # `Markup`, which it is for every html field, and the plain `IN`
            # returns a non-string as it came -- so a rule written with `IN`
            # here would have been a silent no-op at the one site it names.
            # `innerHTML` coerces the wrapper, so what the helper preserves is
            # not needed by the assignment; it is needed because the helper
            # that *prefixes* a `Markup` is this one.
            #
            # **No `OUT` half, because the field already has one.** Nothing is
            # stored by this site, and every write that follows it goes through
            # rules 2 and 5 at the top of this block -- `updateValue` and
            # `_commitChanges`'s `comparisonValue` -- whose strip removes a
            # prefix and otherwise does nothing, so it is a no-op over content
            # that was never prefixed and takes this rule's prefix off again
            # over content that was. The code view's own textarea is where the
            # value goes when the toggle goes the other way, and it reaches
            # `updateValue` too.
            #
            # **What bounds it, and it is wider than #240 and #238 both said.**
            # The button exists only where
            # `codeview: Boolean(odoo.debug && options.codeview)` is true, so
            # debug mode *and* a view that sets the option. Five shipped views
            # in the pinned package set it, on four fields:
            # `res.users.signature` in both of `base`'s user forms
            # (`view_users_form` and Preferences' `view_users_form_simple_modif`),
            # `ir.actions.act_window`'s `help` (#158's field),
            # `mail.template`'s `body_html` and `hr_recruitment`'s send-mail
            # wizard body. The claim in #240 and
            # in the previous postscript that no shipped view sets it was
            # false; the bound that survives is debug mode.
            #
            # The last two are `html_mail`, a subclass of this field, and they
            # are also where a reader can get a false pass: a value whose parse
            # yields a non-empty `<head>` turns `sandboxedPreview` on, the
            # field renders through the readonly `HtmlViewer` (#237's two rules
            # above) and `this.editor` is undefined -- so the toggle runs and
            # this site is never reached. The screen that reaches it with one
            # click is the user signature.
            #
            # The pattern was derived on 2026-10-01 from the pinned `.deb`
            # through Odoo's own serve path. It occurs once each in
            # `web.assets_backend`, `web.assets_web`, `web.assets_web_print`
            # and `project.webclient`, and zero times in the other fifteen
            # bundles the control group serves on this route;
            # `tests/fixtures/bundles/README.md` carries those counts and
            # `tests/test_ingress_code_view_toggle.py` pins them.
            sub_filter 'this.editor.editable.innerHTML=this.value;' 'this.editor.editable.innerHTML=(window.__WOOW_INGRESS_MARKUP_IN_VALUE__||function(h){return h})(this.value);';
            # The `/@` links into the web client (issue #211, U-A2). `/@/<website
            # path>` is Odoo 18's route from a website page into the backend, and
            # it is the one route whose *tail* is itself a website path rather
            # than a URL to fetch. Both builders splice `location.pathname` --
            # already carrying the Ingress prefix -- into that tail:
            #
            #   currentUrl.pathname = `/@${currentUrl.pathname}`
            #
            # so the tail became `/@<PREFIX>/shop/payment`, and then the prefix
            # went on a second time. The shim's `path()` spots a prefix only at
            # position 0, and here it sits at position 2, so the "Edit this
            # content" button on every website page a viewer may edit rendered
            #
            #   href="<PREFIX>/@<PREFIX>/shop/payment"
            #
            # and the web client opened `/api/hassio_ingress/<token>/shop/payment`
            # as a website path. Composing the two steps reproduces the observed
            # literal exactly.
            #
            # The fix is the second of the two the issue names: build the tail
            # from the canonical path and put the prefix on once, at the front,
            # where it belongs. That value is right for every consumer of these
            # two builders and not only for the one that was measured --
            # `backendEditBtnEl.href=currentUrl.href` goes through the shim's
            # anchor-href setter, which returns an already-prefixed path
            # unchanged; redirect.js's two `window.location.replace(currentUrl.href)`
            # calls are writes to `location`, which ADR 0004 says the shim cannot
            # wrap; and the popover hands `browser.open` a **URL object**, which
            # the shim's `window.open` wrapper passes through because it prefixes
            # strings only. Leaving the prefix off and trusting the shim would fix
            # the href and break the other three.
            #
            # nginx reads `${` in a quoted parameter as a variable and has no
            # escape for `$`, so the pattern cannot carry the template literal's
            # interpolation. It stops at the backtick instead, and the replacement
            # re-emits one: what follows -- `${currentUrl.pathname}`; -- is then
            # the argument list of a **tagged template**, so the untouched tail is
            # used rather than stranded. The tag takes the interpolated pathname
            # as its second parameter, the way any tag does.
            #
            # Two ways out with Odoo's own value, both returning `"/@"+p`. With
            # no Ingress prefix there is nothing to move, and this rule is
            # ingress-only in any case. And the prefix belongs on a path of
            # *this* origin only: the popover admits a link by hostname, which
            # says nothing about the port or the scheme, so an absolute link to
            # the add-on's own 8069 listener reaches this builder with a path
            # that has no Ingress prefix and wants none. `path()` refuses the
            # same case with `x.origin===location.origin`; the tag reads
            # `currentUrl` from the scope it is written into, where both
            # builders hold it, because a tag is handed the interpolation and
            # not the URL it came from. redirect.js's `currentUrl` is
            # `location.href`, so it always passes.
            sub_filter 'currentUrl.pathname=`/@' 'currentUrl.pathname=function(s,p){var P="$safe_ingress_path";if(!P||currentUrl.origin!==location.origin)return "/@"+p;if(p.indexOf(P)===0)p=p.slice(P.length)||"/";return P+"/@"+p}`';
            # The popover's guard, which the rewrite above needs. `_onPreviewLinkClick`
            # leaves a link that is already in backend form alone, and it tests
            # the raw pathname against `/@/` -- under Ingress that pathname reads
            # `<PREFIX>/@/shop/x`, the test fails, and the builder above then
            # produces `<PREFIX>/@/@/shop/x`. Before this issue the same input
            # opened `/@<PREFIX>/@/shop/x` at the Home Assistant root, so neither
            # is a regression and both are wrong; the guard is part of getting the
            # link right rather than a second fix. Accepting the prefixed spelling
            # beside the bare one keeps
            # the Ingress surface behaving as the Public one does -- no
            # `preventDefault`, no second window, the anchor followed as it stands.
            # With no prefix the two tests are the same test, which is a no-op.
            #
            # Written with a double-quoted parameter because the expression quotes
            # with `'`. redirect.js carries no such guard and this pattern does not
            # reach it; `tests/test_ingress_at_route_links.py` pins both counts.
            sub_filter "!currentUrl.pathname.startsWith('/@/')" "!(currentUrl.pathname.startsWith('/@/')||currentUrl.pathname.startsWith('$safe_ingress_path/@/'))";
            # Generated rewrites (ADR 0005). The add-on writes this file from
            # its own Rewrite scan; the config-rendering init step creates it
            # empty so a fresh install starts. It holds prefix rules only, and
            # never a prefix the rules above already cover, so the two sets
            # never compete for the same literal. That is a rule about what is
            # generated and not a property of nginx: measured on 1.22.1, two
            # sub_filter patterns that match at the same byte are settled by
            # the order they are written, the earlier one winning even where a
            # later one is longer (issue #158 depends on that, and pins it).
            # The no-overlap rule stays, because the order between a hand-
            # written rule here and a machine-written one inside the include is
            # not something either side gets to reason about.
            include /data/nginx-generated-rewrites.conf;
        }

        location / {
            proxy_pass http://odoo_http;
            proxy_http_version 1.1;
            proxy_set_header Host $http_host;
            proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Host $http_host;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $ingress_proto;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
            proxy_set_header Accept-Encoding "";
            # Odoo backend responses use X-Frame-Options: DENY, which makes
            # the authenticated /odoo document fail before any assets load in
            # Home Assistant's ingress iframe. HA session auth is the frame
            # boundary, so remove this header only on the ingress listener.
            proxy_hide_header X-Frame-Options;
            proxy_request_buffering off;
            proxy_buffering off;
            proxy_read_timeout 720s;
            proxy_send_timeout 720s;

            proxy_redirect ~^(?<odoo_redirect>/.*)$ $safe_ingress_path$odoo_redirect;
            proxy_cookie_path / $safe_ingress_path/;
            proxy_cookie_flags session_id $ingress_cookie_secure httponly samesite=lax;

            sub_filter_once off;
            sub_filter_types application/javascript text/javascript text/css application/json;
            sub_filter 'href="/' 'href="$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'src="/' 'src="$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'action="/' 'action="$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'data-src="/' 'data-src="$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'srcset="/' 'srcset="$safe_ingress_path/';
            # A website form keeps its confirmation target in the page HTML --
            # data-success-mode="redirect" data-success-page="/contactus-thank-you"
            # on Contact Us, "/job-thank-you" on a job application, whatever the
            # user chose on a form built in the editor -- and the form script
            # assigns that root-relative path to the page location once the
            # submit RPC answers. The five rules above are the only attributes
            # rewritten here, the Runtime shim wraps no location assignment (ADR
            # 0004 records that as a decision, not an omission), and the Rewrite
            # scan reads asset bundles and not page HTML, so the value reached
            # the browser bare: the record was created and then the thank-you
            # page was asked of the Home Assistant root, which answered 404
            # (issue #167). The rule belongs to the attribute and not to any one
            # form or path, so it covers every website form on every page. A
            # value that is a same-page anchor (`#thanks`) or an absolute URL
            # matches no byte of it and is left alone.
            #
            # `data-success_page` with an underscore is the same value in the
            # arch a form saved before Odoo 14 left behind, and Odoo 18 still
            # reads it: the script above falls back to it whenever
            # `data-success-mode` is absent. An upgraded database keeps forms in
            # that shape, so the compatibility spelling gets the same rule
            # rather than the same 404.
            #
            # These two inherit the protocol-relative limit of the five rules
            # above -- a plain-string pattern prefixes `//host/path` too, and
            # breaks it -- and here that limit is not covered by "Odoo writes
            # none": the value is what the person editing the form typed into
            # the redirect field. It is still one shape among the file's, fixed
            # for every such rule at once or not at all (issue #166).
            sub_filter 'data-success-page="/' 'data-success-page="$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'data-success_page="/' 'data-success_page="$safe_ingress_path/';
            # Rewritten asset bytes change with ingress releases while Odoo's
            # content hash does not. Cache-bust HTML references so clients do
            # not keep an older transformed bundle after an add-on update.
            sub_filter '.min.js"' '.min.js?woow_ingress=%%INGRESS_CACHE_VERSION%%"';
            sub_filter '.min.css"' '.min.css?woow_ingress=%%INGRESS_CACHE_VERSION%%"';
            sub_filter '&#39;/web/login&#39;' '&#39;$safe_ingress_path/web/login&#39;';
            sub_filter '"translationURL": "/website/' '"translationURL": "$safe_ingress_path/website/';
            # /web/bundle returns JSON objects such as {"src": "/web/assets/..."}.
            # The Website editor may inject those assets into a child iframe
            # whose DOM prototypes are outside the parent runtime shim.
            sub_filter '"src": "/' '"src": "$safe_ingress_path/';
            # Document Layout serializes its preview HTML into JSON, escaping
            # attributes as href=\"...\"/src=\"...\". Prefix only root Odoo
            # assets in those JSON bytes; broad JSON rewriting is unsafe.
            sub_filter 'href=\\"/web/assets/' 'href=\\"$safe_ingress_path/web/assets/';
            sub_filter 'src=\\"/web/assets/' 'src=\\"$safe_ingress_path/web/assets/';
            sub_filter '"icon":"/' '"icon":"$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter '"icon": "/' '"icon": "$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter '"imgurl":"/' '"imgurl":"$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter '"imgurl": "/' '"imgurl": "$safe_ingress_path/';
            # General Settings embeds this exact logo path in get_views XML.
            # Match the path itself so surrounding XML/JSON quote escaping does
            # not affect the ingress-only replacement.
            sub_filter '/base/static/description/settings.png' '$safe_ingress_path/base/static/description/settings.png';
            sub_filter '`/web/webclient/' '`$safe_ingress_path/web/webclient/';
            sub_filter 'url(/' 'url($safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter "url('/" "url('$safe_ingress_path/";
            sub_filter 'url("/' 'url("$safe_ingress_path/';
            # QWeb escapes attribute values with markupsafe, so a website
            # snippet's inline background -- style="background-image:
            # url(&#39;/web/image/...&#39;)" -- reaches the browser with its
            # quote as an HTML entity and matches none of the three rules
            # above: the path went unprefixed and resolved against the Home
            # Assistant root. Each form the escaper can produce gets a rule
            # of its own, keeping the entity exactly as it was. These belong
            # to the generic HTML location only -- asset bundles hold no HTML
            # entities -- and they are written by hand here, so the Rewrite
            # scan, which reads bundles, never learns about page HTML.
            #
            # They inherit the limit of the three above: the pattern is a
            # plain string, so a protocol-relative reference
            # (url(&#39;//cdn/x.png&#39;)) is prefixed too and breaks. No
            # sub_filter can express "root-relative but not //", and no
            # later rule can undo it, because nginx never rescans what a
            # rule replaced. Odoo writes none, and folding the rules into
            # something broader is a change to all seven (issue #166).
            sub_filter 'url(&#39;/' 'url(&#39;$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'url(&#34;/' 'url(&#34;$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'url(&quot;/' 'url(&quot;$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'url(&#x27;/' 'url(&#x27;$safe_ingress_path/';

            sub_filter '<head>' '<head>$ingress_runtime_shim';
        }

        # Action help HTML (issue #158, ADR 0004's 2026-09-28 postscript). A
        # window action's `help` field is HTML held in the database, and Odoo's
        # own help carries root-relative attributes -- the Surveys screen of an
        # empty list shows four `<img src="/survey/static/src/img/...">` sample
        # tiles. The web client reads that field out of this route's JSON-RPC
        # response and hands it to `markup()`, so it reaches the DOM as markup
        # (`innerHTML`) and never passes an API the Runtime shim wraps. Under
        # Ingress the four pictures were asked of the Home Assistant root and
        # answered 404: eight Prefix escapes per screen.
        #
        # The shim is not the fix. ADR 0004 records markup insertion as
        # uncovered *by decision*: the HTML editor and the website editor load
        # and save record content through that same path, so a shim hook would
        # write the Ingress prefix -- which carries the Ingress token -- back
        # into the database. Action help is only ever read through this
        # response and never saved through it, so a Literal rewrite scoped to
        # the route reaches it without reaching the editors.
        #
        # Inside JSON the attribute quote is escaped (`src=\"/survey/...`), so
        # the generic location's raw-quote HTML rules never matched a byte of
        # it; the only escaped-quote rules there cover `/web/assets/`, for the
        # Document Layout preview. Widening those to all JSON is what ADR 0004
        # calls unsafe -- 0.3.34 had to restrict the shim to `text/html` after
        # rewriting JSON blanked that preview -- so the new rules live in an
        # exact-match location of their own and no other JSON response sees
        # them.
        #
        # **This route and no other, even though three deliver help.** The web
        # client runs `markup(action.help)` over the answer of
        # `/web/action/load`, of `/web/action/run` and of
        # `/web/dataset/call_button/<model>/<method>` alike. Only the first is
        # safe to rewrite, and the difference is not the help: it is the rest
        # of the action dict.
        #
        # `/web/action/load` answers with a **stored** `ir.actions.act_window`
        # record. Its `help` is database HTML the client only ever displays,
        # and its `context` is the static string a developer wrote into the
        # action definition. Nothing in that response is content the client
        # will hand back.
        #
        # The other two answer with an action **computed at call time**, and a
        # computed action carries record content in its `context` as wizard
        # defaults. `marketing_card`'s `action_share()` button is the shipped
        # example: it returns an `act_window` whose
        # `context.default_body_arch` is a mail body holding
        # `<img src="/web/image/card.campaign/<id>/image_preview">` and
        # `<a href="/cards/<id>/preview">`. Prefix those and the mailing opens
        # pre-filled with the Ingress prefix, and the first save writes the
        # Supervisor token into `mailing.mailing.body_arch`. That is the
        # database-poisoning this entire decision exists to avoid, reached by
        # one more step. A server action's returned dict is user-authored
        # Python and is at least as open.
        #
        # So the rule is: **rewrite a response only when everything in it is
        # something the client displays and never returns.** Help delivered
        # through the other two routes keeps escaping, and that is the smaller
        # harm; it needs a fix that can tell a field from a field, which a
        # byte-level `sub_filter` cannot. `/web/action/load_breadcrumbs`
        # answers with display names only and needs nothing.
        #
        # Everything before `# Action help, escaped-quote rules` is every
        # directive of `location /` above, in its order and with only its
        # comments left behind: proxy headers, cookie path and flags,
        # X-Frame-Options removal, buffering, timeouts, and every rule it
        # carries. Ingress behaviour for this route differs from the generic
        # location only by the added rules, and `test_ingress_action_help.py`
        # compares the two directive lists, so later drift in either fails
        # rather than quietly changing the route.
        location = /web/action/load {
            proxy_pass http://odoo_http;
            proxy_http_version 1.1;
            proxy_set_header Host $http_host;
            proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Host $http_host;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $ingress_proto;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
            proxy_set_header Accept-Encoding "";
            proxy_hide_header X-Frame-Options;
            proxy_request_buffering off;
            proxy_buffering off;
            proxy_read_timeout 720s;
            proxy_send_timeout 720s;

            proxy_redirect ~^(?<odoo_redirect>/.*)$ $safe_ingress_path$odoo_redirect;
            proxy_cookie_path / $safe_ingress_path/;
            proxy_cookie_flags session_id $ingress_cookie_secure httponly samesite=lax;

            sub_filter_once off;
            sub_filter_types application/javascript text/javascript text/css application/json;
            sub_filter 'href="/' 'href="$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'src="/' 'src="$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'action="/' 'action="$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'data-src="/' 'data-src="$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'srcset="/' 'srcset="$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'data-success-page="/' 'data-success-page="$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'data-success_page="/' 'data-success_page="$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter '.min.js"' '.min.js?woow_ingress=%%INGRESS_CACHE_VERSION%%"';
            sub_filter '.min.css"' '.min.css?woow_ingress=%%INGRESS_CACHE_VERSION%%"';
            sub_filter '&#39;/web/login&#39;' '&#39;$safe_ingress_path/web/login&#39;';
            sub_filter '"translationURL": "/website/' '"translationURL": "$safe_ingress_path/website/';
            sub_filter '"src": "/' '"src": "$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'href=\\"/web/assets/' 'href=\\"$safe_ingress_path/web/assets/';
            sub_filter 'src=\\"/web/assets/' 'src=\\"$safe_ingress_path/web/assets/';
            sub_filter '"icon":"/' '"icon":"$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter '"icon": "/' '"icon": "$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter '"imgurl":"/' '"imgurl":"$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter '"imgurl": "/' '"imgurl": "$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter '/base/static/description/settings.png' '$safe_ingress_path/base/static/description/settings.png';
            sub_filter '`/web/webclient/' '`$safe_ingress_path/web/webclient/';
            sub_filter 'url(/' 'url($safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter "url('/" "url('$safe_ingress_path/";
            sub_filter 'url("/' 'url("$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'url(&#39;/' 'url(&#39;$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'url(&#34;/' 'url(&#34;$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'url(&quot;/' 'url(&quot;$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'url(&#x27;/' 'url(&#x27;$safe_ingress_path/';

            sub_filter '<head>' '<head>$ingress_runtime_shim';

            # Action help, escaped-quote rules, over the five URL attributes
            # the generic location's raw-quote rules cover. The two rules it
            # also carries for a website form's confirmation target
            # (`data-success-page`, and the pre-Odoo-14 `data-success_page`,
            # issue #167) get no escaped-quote counterpart: that value belongs
            # to page HTML, and a stored action's help never holds a form.
            #
            # The identity rules come first because they have to: sub_filter
            # settles two patterns that match at the same byte by the order
            # they are written, the earlier one winning even where a later one
            # is longer. Written ahead of the general rule, each identity rule
            # takes a URL that already carries the prefix and puts it back
            # unchanged, so nothing is prefixed twice. The escaped
            # `/web/assets/` rules above are ahead of both and keep answering
            # for the Document Layout preview, with the result the general
            # rules would have given anyway.
            #
            # Two limits are inherited from the raw-quote counterparts, because
            # a sub_filter pattern is a plain string and not a parser:
            #
            #   * a protocol-relative reference (`src=\"//cdn/x.png`) is
            #     prefixed too, and breaks;
            #   * `srcset` is a *list*, so only its first candidate is behind
            #     the attribute opener -- `srcset=\"/a 1x, /b 2x` prefixes
            #     `/a` and leaves `/b` escaping.
            #
            # Odoo's shipped action help writes neither shape, and both are
            # fixed for every such rule in this file at once or not at all
            # (issue #166).
            sub_filter 'href=\\"$safe_ingress_path/' 'href=\\"$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'src=\\"$safe_ingress_path/' 'src=\\"$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'action=\\"$safe_ingress_path/' 'action=\\"$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'data-src=\\"$safe_ingress_path/' 'data-src=\\"$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'srcset=\\"$safe_ingress_path/' 'srcset=\\"$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'href=\\"/' 'href=\\"$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'src=\\"/' 'src=\\"$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'action=\\"/' 'action=\\"$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'data-src=\\"/' 'data-src=\\"$safe_ingress_path/';
            sub_filter 'srcset=\\"/' 'srcset=\\"$safe_ingress_path/';
        }
    }
}
